{
"Q33": {
 "verification": {"status": "VERIFIED", "changes": [], "residualConcerns": "", "spotCheckPriority": "normal"}
},
"Q34": {
 "top": {
  "screeningLimitations": "The sample was 11 resistance-trained men tested after one single-exercise protocol with no injured athletes or women, so the findings may not transfer to team-sport athletes or other training types. The correlations are within-person, so they do not show that the same PRS score means the same level of recovery across people."
 },
 "verification": {"status": "CORRECTED", "changes": ["screeningLimitations: described the sample as 'recreationally trained men' -> 'resistance-trained men' ('Eleven apparently healthy, resistance-trained men'; all reported 5+ y of training)"], "residualConcerns": "", "spotCheckPriority": "normal"}
},
"Q35": {
 "verification": {"status": "VERIFIED", "changes": [], "residualConcerns": "", "spotCheckPriority": "normal"}
},
"Q36": {
 "top": {
  "evidenceSummary": "A PRISMA systematic review and multilevel meta-analysis of overground repeated-sprint training (RST) in team sport athletes aged 16 or over, covering 215 publications, 176 of them meta-analysed (908 samples). Pooled estimates across all protocols were: end-set blood lactate 10.7 mmol/L (90% CI 10.1 to 11.3), peak heart rate 182 bpm, session RPE 6.5 deciMax and sprint decrement 5.0% (90% CI 4.7 to 5.3). Programming effects were tested by meta-regression against a reference protocol of 6 x 30 m sprints with 20 s passive rest, with 10 m longer sprints raising blood lactate by 2.7 mmol/L and 10 s longer rest lowering it by 1.1 mmol/L.",
  "screeningLimitations": "The pooled estimates describe healthy, non-injured team sport athletes performing overground running, so they do not transfer to other sports, injured athletes or treadmill and cycle sprints. Many moderator effects were inconclusive or compatible with both trivial and substantial effects, CMJ height and creatine kinase could not be pooled, and average VO2 was pooled from only 6 studies."
 },
 "paper": {
  "findings": "Pooled estimates across protocols were average heart rate 163 bpm (90% CI 154 to 171), peak heart rate 182 bpm, end-set blood lactate 10.7 mmol/L, session RPE 6.5 deciMax, best sprint time 5.52 s and sprint decrement 5.0% (90% CI 4.7 to 5.3). Compared with the 6 x 30 m, 20 s passive rest reference, shuttle-based sprints increased average and best sprint times (1.42 s and 1.55 s slower) with a trivial effect on session RPE. Two more repetitions per set had trivial effects on most outcomes. A 10 m longer sprint increased blood lactate by 2.7 mmol/L and sprint decrement by 1.7%. A 10 s longer rest lowered blood lactate by 1.1 mmol/L and sprint decrement by 1.4%, with trivial effects on peak heart rate and session RPE. Active rest and rest modality effects were largely inconclusive.",
  "limitations": "CMJ height, creatine kinase and peak heart rate as a percentage of maximum had too few comparable samples to be meta-analysed, and moderators could not be tested for average heart rate or VO2. Many moderator effects had confidence intervals spanning trivial and substantial effects, and part of the variation reflected factors not modelled, such as age, fitness, sex, number of sets and data collection methods. Some values were digitised from figures or estimated rest times, samples were removed when authors could not clarify protocols, and the review excluded injured athletes and sprints at 30 C or above."
 },
 "verification": {"status": "CORRECTED", "changes": [
  "evidenceSummary: pooled values were labelled as estimates 'for a reference protocol of 6 x 30 m sprints with 20 s passive rest' -> pooled across all protocols; the 6 x 30 m protocol is only the meta-regression reference ('When compared with a reference protocol of 6 x 30 m straight-line sprints...')",
  "screeningLimitations: said VO2 could not be pooled -> average VO2 was pooled from 6 studies (Table 3: VO2avg 6 studies, 42.4); CMJ and creatine kinase were not pooled",
  "paper.findings: 'pooled reference estimates' -> pooled estimates across protocols, and moderator effects tied to the reference protocol",
  "paper.limitations: claimed missing data 'were not supplied by the authors and were estimated' -> samples were removed when authors did not respond ('If authors did not respond, samples were removed from the meta-analysis'); figure data were digitised",
  "paper.limitations: 'heterogeneity was large and mostly unexplained' -> 'a proportion of the variation ... can be explained by factors other than the programming variables investigated'",
  "paper.limitations: said VO2 was not pooled -> moderators could not be tested for average heart rate and VO2 ('insufficient number of samples to determine the moderating effects ... on average heart rate and VO2')"
 ], "residualConcerns": "", "spotCheckPriority": "high"}
},
"Q37": {
 "top": {
  "populations": ["Adult / Recreational", "Healthy Athletes"]
 },
 "verification": {"status": "CORRECTED", "changes": [
  "populations: 'Professional / Elite' -> 'Adult / Recreational'; the squad is a semiprofessional Spanish Third Division team ('4th national category ... which corresponded to TIER 3'), not professional or elite"
 ], "residualConcerns": "Taxonomy has no semiprofessional level; Adult / Recreational is the closest fit. Sex is implied ('he') but not explicitly stated, so no sex tag was added.", "spotCheckPriority": "high"}
},
"Q38": {
 "verification": {"status": "VERIFIED", "changes": [], "residualConcerns": "", "spotCheckPriority": "normal"}
},
"Q39": {
 "top": {
  "primaryDomain": "Monitoring & Technology",
  "domains": ["Monitoring & Technology", "Training & Performance"]
 },
 "paper": {
  "limitations": "Development and validation used the same public dataset, with no external validation; only complete records were used, so the final models included 176, 141 and 185 players, and the abstract gives the line group as 211 while the Methods and Table 4 give 221. The outcome is draft selection, not later CFL performance, and the score covers combine measures only, not skill, game performance or injury. Some of the reported coefficient confidence intervals and AUC values are presented inconsistently in the text and tables, so the exact figures should be read with caution.",
  "practicalImplications": "Coaches and players aiming for the CFL can use the score tables to see the height, mass and athletic values linked to being drafted for each position group. The 40-yard sprint was the strongest and most consistent predictor in every group, while vertical jump, bench press and 3-cone terms were not statistically significant, so those targets carry less weight. The tables describe draft selection rather than skill, so they indicate a physical profile to work toward, not a complete standard."
 },
 "verification": {"status": "CORRECTED", "changes": [
  "primaryDomain: 'Training & Performance' -> 'Monitoring & Technology' (main contribution is a scoring tool built from combine tests, which the field rules place under Monitoring & Technology)",
  "paper.practicalImplications: advised setting 'targets for sprint, jump and bench press work' -> jump, bench press and 3-cone coefficients were not significant in Table 5; only 40-yd sprint (p<0.001 in all groups), plus height and mass in some groups, were significant",
  "paper.limitations: added complete-case model sizes (Table 5 n = 176, 141, 185) and the line sample conflict (abstract 'n = 211' vs Methods and Table 4 'n = 221')"
 ], "residualConcerns": "Own DOI is not printed in the text, so the record stays DEGRADED until the DOI is verified externally.", "spotCheckPriority": "high"}
},
"Q40": {
 "paper": {
  "methods": "Repeated-measures observational study of 23 male NCAA Division I football players, with one assessment per week on a randomly allocated weekday over 10 weeks. Each weekly assessment included a fasted fingertip blood sample for FORT and FORD (used to compute the oxidative stress index), an overnight urine sample for cortisol and three CMJs on force platforms (flight time, RSImod and concentric impulse), and players also completed a daily morning wellness questionnaire for sleep, soreness and fatigue. Linear mixed models with random player intercepts were run, with effects standardized to a 2 within-subject SD change and interpreted with magnitude-based inference.",
  "limitations": "One team of 23 players in one season, with 39 models run and no correction for multiple testing. The abstract's effect-size signs conflict with the text (FORD-sleep is reported as +0.42 but described as a negative association), its conclusion that soreness is linked with increased cortisol is not supported by the Discussion, and the Discussion says cortisol was associated only with FORT even though the Results report moderate cortisol associations with concentric impulse and sleep. Samples were randomized across training days, so acute daily load effects were not controlled, and two authors consult for the company that supplied the FORT/FORD tests.",
  "practicalImplications": "Routine subjective soreness scores can be collected cheaply and were associated with CMJ performance in this squad, so they may be worth including in monitoring. The FORT/FORD test is costly and its associations were small, so it should be used to support other measures rather than alone.",
  "athleteDev": "Tracking soreness alongside CMJ may help staff notice when collegiate players are less ready to train. The study did not test whether adjusting load based on these measures improves performance or adaptation."
 },
 "verification": {"status": "CORRECTED", "changes": [
  "paper.methods: implied the wellness questionnaire was weekly -> it was daily ('a daily subjective wellness questionnaire'; 2086 responses)",
  "paper.practicalImplications: 'Weekly subjective soreness scores' -> routine (questionnaires were daily)",
  "paper.limitations: added the cortisol conflict ('Other than the expected association with FORT, cortisol was not associated with any of the other variables' vs Results 'Moderate positive associations ... cortisol-concentric impulse and sleep') and the Orreco consultancy disclosure",
  "paper.athleteDev: removed 'which can guide daily load decisions', which the study did not test"
 ], "residualConcerns": "", "spotCheckPriority": "normal"}
}
}

